, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Scribes rarely marked vowel length, so a long-vowel word (sott 'fool') and a short-vowel near-homophone (sot 'soot') could be confused; Byrhtferth's Handbook cites this as a real source of comic mispronunciation when delivering an insult, doubly embarrassing because sott was itself a fashionable, possibly French-influenced loan."}, {"id": "established_086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02_descriptive_title_word_order", "label": "Descriptive-title word order and bynames/epithets", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02_ealdorman_vs_eorl", "label": "Ealdorman vs. eorl: rank system in flux, with a Norse-contact loan", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Eorl originally meant 'brave man, warrior' in poetry but gained currency as a formal rank specifically because it sounded like Old Norse jarl, Cnut's own native word for the same rank; the two languages were mutually comprehensible enough for the overlap to read as a natural borrowing rather than a foreign imposition."}, {"id": "established_086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02_manuscript_recension_c_vs_d", "label": "Explicit manuscript/recension variation: Chronicle C vs. D", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02_roman_calendar_system", "label": "The Roman calendar system (ides, nones, kalends)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02_grammatical_gender_article", "label": "Grammatical gender and the three-way definite article", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02_inflected_infinitive_enne", "label": "The inflected (dative) infinitive -enne", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02_accusative_case_ne_ending", "label": "The accusative case: marking the object, and the -ne ending", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02_correlative_da_da", "label": "Correlative \u00f0a...\u00f0a / \u00feonne...\u00feonne (when...then)", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02_impersonal_verbs_methought", "label": "Impersonal verbs: me \u00feuhte 'it seemed to me' vs. me m\u00e6tte 'I dreamed'", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "established/086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "established_086_teach_yourself_old_english_pt02_frith_legal_sacred", "label": "Fri\u00f0 as a legal/sacred concept", "file_type": "c